findings.evidence:
File: pkg/jwtmanager/jwtmanager.go, function decodeAndDecompressTokenString(), lines 187-210:

```go
func decodeAndDecompressTokenString(encgzipss string) string {
    var gzipss []byte
    gzipss, err := base64.URLEncoding.DecodeString(encgzipss)
    if err != nil {
        log.Debugf("Error in Base64decode: %v", err)
    }

    breader := bytes.NewReader(gzipss)
    zr, err := gzip.NewReader(breader)
    if err != nil {
        log.Debugf("Error reading gzip data: %v", err)
        return ""
    }
    if err := zr.Close(); err != nil {  // <-- WRONG: zr.Close() called here
        log.Debugf("Error decoding token: %v", err)
    }
    ss, _ := io.ReadAll(zr)  // <-- WRONG: Reading from closed reader
    return string(ss)
}
```

The issue is on lines 203-206: zr.Close() is called BEFORE io.ReadAll(zr). This means io.ReadAll() attempts to read from an already-closed reader, which will return empty bytes or an error. The decompressed token data is never actually read, causing the function to return an empty string. This directly impacts JWT token validation since the JWT parser cannot parse an empty string.

Defaulting to compress: true in .defaults.yml means this bug affects default deployments.

findings.recommendation:
Fix the operation order in the decodeAndDecompressTokenString function. The correct order is: decompress → read → close. Implement with proper defer for resource cleanup:

```go
func decodeAndDecompressTokenString(encgzipss string) string {
    var gzipss []byte
    gzipss, err := base64.URLEncoding.DecodeString(encgzipss)
    if err != nil {
        log.Debugf("Error in Base64decode: %v", err)
        return ""
    }

    breader := bytes.NewReader(gzipss)
    zr, err := gzip.NewReader(breader)
    if err != nil {
        log.Debugf("Error reading gzip data: %v", err)
        return ""
    }
    defer func() {
        if err := zr.Close(); err != nil {
            log.Debugf("Error closing gzip reader: %v", err)
        }
    }()

    ss, err := io.ReadAll(zr)  // Read BEFORE closing
    if err != nil {
        log.Debugf("Error reading decompressed data: %v", err)
        return ""
    }
    return string(ss)
}
```

Also add unit tests that specifically test the jwt.compress feature with actual compressed tokens to ensure the round-trip (compress → decompress) works correctly.
